<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="biostats-notes-7-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Inference on Correlation </title>
    
    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Find the standard error in the slope for regression lines. </p></li>
            <li><p> Conduct hypothesis tests on the slope of a regression line. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-standard-error">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <alert>standard error</alert> in the slope of the linear regression line is <md> SE = \sqrt{\frac{SSE}{(n-2)S_{XX}}} </md> where we compute <md> SE = \sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \hat{y}_i). </md> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the SE in the slope of the regression line for the bivariate data 
            </p>
            <tabular>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><m>X</m></cell><cell><m>Y</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>0</cell><cell>82</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>1</cell><cell>80</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>2</cell><cell>78</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>3</cell><cell>79</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>4</cell><cell>75</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                given that its regression line is <m>\hat{y} = -1.5\hat{x} + 81.8</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                We first compute the <q>expected value</q> of each input using the regression line. Then we can compute 
                <md>
                    SSE = (82 - 81.8)^2 + \cdots + (75 - 75.8)^2 \approx 4.3
                </md>
                Now we compute <md>S_{XX} = (0 - 2)^2 + \cdots + (4 - 2)^2 = 10.</md> Therefore, <md>
                    SE = \sqrt{\frac{SSE}{(n-2)S_{XX}}} \approx 0.379.
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
        From this point forward, we are essentially just conducting a hypothesis test on a mean, except that we have 1 less degree of freedom - considering we know the slope. We use the <m>T</m> distribution with <m>n-2</m> degrees of freedom. 
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Work through activity 17 problem 2.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
</section>
